      en: "Chitwan National Park, Nepal's first national park, was established in 1973 and granted UNESCO World Heritage status in 1984. Located in the subtropical Inner Terai lowlands of south-central Nepal, it covers 952.63 square kilometers. The park's landscape consists of dense sal forests, tall grasslands, and riverine habitats. It is globally renowned for its protection of the one-horned rhinoceros and the Bengal tiger. Visitors can embark on jungle safaris to spot a wide array of wildlife, including elephants, sloth bears, leopards, and various deer species. The park is also an ornithologist's paradise, with over 540 recorded bird species. The Rapti and Narayani river systems within the park are a critical habitat for the endangered gharial crocodile."
